# THE RECORD FORM — copy this to `.row-currency` at the root of the repository
# that OWNS your working record.
#
# Its presence is the declaration. It switches on
# scripts/hooks/guard-row-currency-commits.sh, and deleting it stands the
# mechanism down in a diff somebody can see.
#
# WHAT PROBLEM THIS SOLVES
# ------------------------
# A working list has rows. Rows describe things. Things change. Nothing
# connects the two, so a row goes on saying what was true last week and the
# only detector is a person who happens to read it.
#
# The row's fix is always a manual step after the merge, and a step nobody is
# forced to take is a step that gets skipped exactly when the day is busiest.
#
# HOW IT IS FIXED
# ---------------
# Each governed row's last cell carries a warrant: a status word, and every
# path that row describes pinned to the object id it had when the row was
# written.
#
#     | 4.2 | prose about the work | **State:** `OPEN` — `<repo>/src/parser.rs`@`0a1b2c3d4e5f` |
#
# The next landing recomputes those ids out of the tree the commit is about to
# create. If one has moved and the row has not, the landing is refused and the
# refusal prints the warrant to paste.
#
# THERE IS NO RE-STAMP COMMAND. That is the point: a tool that refreshes the
# pin would let the obligation be discharged by a keystroke, with nobody
# reading the sentence beside it.
#
# ONLY AT A LANDING: the main checkout, an attached HEAD, `git commit` or
# `git merge`. Branch work in a linked worktree is a proposal and is never
# blocked.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT SAY WHERE THE RECORD IS. That, and the artifact-root map,
# are declared once in `.ceo-todos` and read from there. A second copy of a
# fact is a second thing to keep true.

# Which sections of the record carry governed rows. Space separated. Sections
# that are already governed by the CEO-TODOs contract usually do not belong
# here: that contract already checks their one claim (the artifact exists).
ROW_SECTIONS="3"

# The status vocabulary. Spelling is checked; meaning is yours. The token lives
# inside the warrant, so it cannot be re-pinned without being re-typed.
ROW_STATUS_TOKENS="OPEN BUILT BOUNDED BLOCKED-ON-RICH CLOSED"

# Terminal states: finished work. Exempt from the pin, because a later change
# to the work cannot make "this finished" false — and named in a note on every
# run, because a finished row is not open work and should leave the page.
ROW_TERMINAL_TOKENS="CLOSED"

# OPTIONAL, and OFF until you write it. Which of the governed sections must also
# carry a `**Headline:**` warrant — the row's own first sentence, digested, plus
# either the command that settles it and that command's output, or the word
# `unverified` and what would settle it.
#
#   **Headline:** `4f2a9c1e83bd` — `./scripts/some-suite.sh` → `37 passed, 0 failed`
#   **Headline:** `4f2a9c1e83bd` — unverified "one uninterrupted run of the suite, read at its final counts line"
#
# WHY YOU WOULD WANT IT. `ROW_SECTIONS` above pins the WORK a row points at. On
# 2026-09-06 sixteen rows of one record were re-derived and found overtaken, and
# ELEVEN OF THE SIXTEEN HAD A MATCHING PIN: the file had not moved, the world
# had, and the row's bold first sentence — the part that gets quoted into a
# brief — was false while every warrant on the page was green. The hex is a
# digest of the rest of the row, so appending a correction underneath a headline,
# or re-stamping a pin above it, refuses the next landing until a person has
# looked at that first sentence again. Re-stamping is not re-reading.
#
# THE COST, measured over 124 real commits to the record this was built for: 86
# landings would have fired, 157 rows in total, median ONE row per landing — and
# every one of those landings was already editing the row it named. Adoption is a
# one-time warrant on every governed row, exactly as ROW_SECTIONS was.
#
# Must name only sections that are already in ROW_SECTIONS. Re-run the recorded
# commands with scripts/row-headline-verify.sh; deliberately never in a hook.
# ROW_HEADLINE_SECTIONS="3"

# OPTIONAL, and 0 unless you say otherwise. Whether a governed row that carries
# NO headline warrant at all is a refusal.
#
# It is 0 by default for the reason PREMISE_REQUIRED is, and the reason is not
# timidity. A record adopts this contract with rows already on it, and in this
# engine some of those rows are written BY A MACHINE — the mechanical sweep
# appends a row to the record on its own, at a turn end, and it does not know
# how to state a headline warrant. A check that refused every landing the moment
# the declaration went in would be deleted the same day, which is the failure
# this project has three recorded instances of in one day.
#
# So at 0: a row WITH a warrant is held to it from the first character, and a
# row WITHOUT one is COUNTED in the HC census and NAMED in a note at every
# landing. The teeth are one line away when the page is ready for them.
# ROW_HEADLINE_REQUIRED="0"

# OPTIONAL. The words that turn a number in a commit message into a claim about
# an item, so that "we closed item 4.2" must come with a change to 4.2's row.
# An allowlist, because the set of words that can precede a decimal number is
# unbounded and the set of ways your team names an item is not. Omit it to take
# the engine's default.
ROW_CLAIM_WORDS="item items open-item open-items decision decisions"
